88, 'speaker': 'B'}]}, {'confidence': 0.96214625, 'end': 4348660, 'speaker': 'A', 'start': 4340758, 'text': 'And then Bert asks, how would language models using predictive coding differ from those using transformers?', 'words': [{'text': 'And', 'start': 4340758, 'end': 4341024, 'confidence': 0.78, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'then', 'start': 4341062, 'end': 4341312, 'confidence': 0.9998, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'Bert', 'start': 4341366, 'end': 4341852, 'confidence': 0.89339, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'asks,', 'start': 4341916, 'end': 4342640, 'confidence': 0.99208, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'how', 'start': 4343380, 'end': 4343744, 'confidence': 0.99962, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'would', 'start': 4343782, 'end': 4344128, 'confidence': 0.99993, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'language', 'start': 4344214, 'end': 4344684, 'confidence': 0.99997, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'models', 'start': 4344732, 'end': 4345224, 'confidence': 0.73124, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'using', 'start': 4345292, 'end': 4345524, 'confidence': 0.99983, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'predictive', 'start': 4345562, 'end': 4346016, 'confidence': 0.99922, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'coding', 'start': 4346048, 'end': 4346464, 'confidence': 0.99967, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'differ', 'start': 4346512, 'end': 4346772, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'from', 'start': 4346826, 'end': 4346996, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'those', 'start': 4347018, 'end': 4347204, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'using', 'start': 4347242, 'end': 4347540, 'confidence': 0.99999, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'transformers?', 'start': 4347610, 'end': 4348660, 'confidence': 0.9996, 'speaker': 'A'}]}, {'confidence': 0.9474761052631568, 'end': 4462070, 'speaker': 'B', 'start': 4352600, 'text': "Okay, I think that actually, if I would have to build today a language model using predictive coding, I would still use transformers. So the idea is that, for example, if you have, let's say, this hierarchical graphical model or this hierarchical Bayesian network, I've defined in the very first slides one arrow to encode a function, which is the linear map. Okay? So one arrow was simply the multiplication of the vector encoded in the latent variables times this weight matrix that you can then make nonlinear and things like that. But that can be actually something much more complex. The function encoded in the arrow can be a convolution, can be an attention. So actually, how I would do it, I will still use the which is actually the way we did it in the Oxford group last year, is that we had exactly the structure. Every arrow is a transformer now. So one is the attention mechanism and the next one is the feed forward network. As transformers. Basically, the only difference that you have is that those variables, you want to compute the posterior and you make those posteriors independent via minfield approximation. So basically, you follow all the steps that allow you to converge to the variation of. Free energy of creative coding. But the way you compute predictions and the way you send signals back is done via transformer. So I will still use transformers in general. I mean, they work so well that I don't think that we can be arrogant and say, oh no, I'm going to do it better via a purely predictive coding way. Structure learning is a way, but will still approximate transformers anyway.", 'words': [{'text': 'Okay,', 'start': 4352600, 'end': 4353060, 'confidence': 0.9543, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'I', 'start': 4353130, 'end': 4353316, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'think', 'start': 4353338, 'end': 4353476, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'that', 'start': 4353498, 'end': 4353684, 'confidence': 0.99994, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'actually,', 'start': 4353722, 'end': 4354404, 'confidence': 0.9998, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'if', 'start': 4354602, 'end': 4354964, 'confidence': 0.99998, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'I', 'start': 4355002, 'end': 4355156