lts': [], 'summary': {}}, 'iab_categories_result': {'status': 'success', 'results': [{'text': "Hello and welcome. It's Active Inference guest stream number 51.1 on July 28, 2023. We are here with Tomaso Salvatore and we will be having a presentation and a discussion on the recent work Causal Inference via Predictive Coding. So thanks so much for joining. For those who are watching Live, feel free to write questions in the live chat and off to you. Thank you. Thank you very much, Daniel, for inviting me.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.012134267948567867, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Business>MarketingAndAdvertising'}, {'relevance': 0.00667122146114707, 'label': 'Religion&Spirituality>Astrology'}, {'relevance': 0.0057240696623921394, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>Internet>SocialNetworking'}, {'relevance': 0.0019004812929779291, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Business>Sales'}, {'relevance': 0.0015936738345772028, 'label': 'Science>SpaceAndAstronomy'}, {'relevance': 0.001032077125273645, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>Vaccines'}, {'relevance': 0.0009170115808956325, 'label': 'Shopping>SalesAndPromotions'}, {'relevance': 0.0007523243548348546, 'label': 'Hobbies&Interests>ContentProduction'}, {'relevance': 0.0006515947752632201, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>Internet>Search'}, {'relevance': 0.0005172128439880908, 'label': 'Hobbies&Interests>ParanormalPhenomena'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 19260, 'end': 53370}, 'sentences_idx_start': 0, 'sentences_idx_end': 6}, {'text': "Always been a big fan of the channel and I've been watching a lot of videos, so I'm quite excited to be here and be the one speaking this time. So I'm going to talk about this recent preprint that I put out, which has been the work of the last couple of months. And it's a collaboration with Luca Binketti, Amin, Mccarrak Berenmille and Thomas Lukasiavic.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.7445067167282104, 'label': 'BooksAndLiterature>ArtAndPhotographyBooks'}, {'relevance': 0.29512137174606323, 'label': 'FineArt>DigitalArts'}, {'relevance': 0.15587583184242249, 'label': 'Hobbies&Interests>ContentProduction'}, {'relevance': 0.050480883568525314, 'label': 'Style&Fashion>BodyArt'}, {'relevance': 0.04977507144212723, 'label': 'BooksAndLiterature>ComicsAndGraphicNovels'}, {'relevance': 0.032561566680669785, 'label': 'FineArt>Design'}, {'relevance': 0.022687867283821106, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>DesktopPublishing'}, {'relevance': 0.017515629529953003, 'label': 'Movies>DocumentaryMovies'}, {'relevance': 0.012747888453304768, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Industries>PublishingIndustry'}, {'relevance': 0.007478882092982531, 'label': 'Hobbies&Interests>ContentProduction>FreelanceWriting'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 54320, 'end': 80800}, 'sentences_idx_start': 7, 'sentences_idx_end': 9}, {'text': "And it's basically a joint work between Verses, which is the company I work for, the University of Oxford and Teovien. So during this talk, this is basically the outline of the talk, I will start talking about what predictive coding is and give an introduction of what it is, a brief historical introduction, why I think it's important to study predictive coding, even, for example, from the machine learning perspective. I will then provide a small intro to what causal inference is.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.02227143570780754, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.0044409967958927155, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>Internet>Search'}, {'relevance': 0.00418482068926096, 'label': 'Science'}, {'relevance': 0.003406231291592121, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>DataStorageAndWarehousing'}, {'relevance': 0.0029750890098512173, 'label': 'Education>EducationalAssessment'}, {'relevance': 0.0023103037383407354, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.002069334266707301, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>Databases'}, {'relevance': 0.0013682524440810084, 'label': 'Religion&Spirituality>Astrology'}, {'relevance': 0.0011436892673373222, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>Internet>SocialNetworking'}, {'relevance': 0.001117600011639297, 'label': 'Education>EducationalAssessment>StandardizedTesting'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 81700, 'end': 124130}, 'sentences_idx_start': 10, 'sentences_idx_end': 12}, {'text': 'And once we have all those informations together, I will then discuss why I wrote this paper, what was basically the research question that inspired me and the other collaborators and present the main results, which are how to perform inference so intervention and counterfactual inference, and how to learn the causal structures from a given data set using predictive coding. And then I will of course conclude with some small summary and some discussion on why I believe this work can be impactful and some future directions. So what is predative coding?', 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.0038464595563709736, 'label': 'Science'}, {'relevance': 0.0027869760524481535, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Business>MarketingAndAdvertising'}, {'relevance': 0.0012963913613930345, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>DataStorageAndWarehousing'}, {'relevance': 0.0012264607939869165, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>PersonalInvesting>Options'}, {'relevance': 0.0010475338203832507, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>Databases'}, {'relevance': 0.0007273416849784553, 'label': 'Religion&Spirituality>Astrology'}, {'relevance': 0.0007032445864751935, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>Internet>Search'}, {'relevance': 0.0006940762395970523, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>PersonalInvesting'}, {'relevance': 0.0005904869758524001, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>Internet>SocialNetworking'}, {'relevance': 0.0005552332731895149, 'label': 'Education>EducationalAssessment>StandardizedTesting'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 125700, 'end': 173036}, 'sentences_idx_start': 13, 'sentences_idx_end': 15}, {'text': 'predative coding is in general famous for being a neuroscience inspired learning method. So a theory of how information processing in the brain works and brain, formally speaking, the theory of creative coding can be described as basically having a hierarchical structure of neurons in the brain. And you have two different families of neurons in the brain. The first family is the one in charging of sending prediction information. So neurons in a specific level of the hierarchy send information and predict the activity of the level below.', 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.09720103442668915, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.03378966823220253, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.01177278719842434, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.011376335285604, 'label': 'FamilyAndRelationships>Parenting>ParentingBabiesAndToddlers'}, {'relevance': 0.0071785226464271545, 'label': 'Hobbies&Interests>ArtsAndCrafts>DrawingAndSketching'}, {'relevance': 0.004730232059955597, 'label': 'FineArt>DigitalArts'}, {'relevance': 0.00466260127723217, 'label': 'Education>LanguageLearning'}, {'relevance': 0.0036688032560050488, 'label': 'Technology&Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.003096748609095812, 'label': 'FineArt>Design'}, {'relevance': 0.0028696667868644, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>EducationalVideoGames'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 173228, 'end': 215010}, 'sentences_idx_start': 16, 'sentences_idx_end': 20}, {'text': 'And the second family of neuron is that of error neurons. And the error neurons, they send prediction error information up the hierarchy. So one level predicts the activity of the level below. This prediction has some mismatch which was actually going on in the level below, and information about the prediction error gets sent up the hierarchy.', 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.04418446123600006, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>BrainAndNervousSystemDisorders'}, {'relevance': 0.01685916632413864, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>EndocrineAndMetabolicDiseases>HormonalDisorders'}, {'relevance': 0.000998775358311832, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.0006532060797326267, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>Injuries'}, {'relevance': 0.0002814938488882035, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Robotics'}, {'relevance': 0.00027947218040935695, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>EndocrineAndMetabolicDiseases'}, {'relevance': 0.0002599652507342398, 'label': 'Science>Genetics'}, {'relevance': 0.0002350840368308127, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>SleepDisorders'}, {'relevance': 0.000225192925427109, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>VirtualReality'}, {'relevance': 0.0001933373714564368, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>Internet>InternetOfThings'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 215940, 'end': 241240}, 'sentences_idx_start': 21, 'sentences_idx_end': 24}, {'text': 'However, predictive coding was actually not burned as a neuroscience, as a theory from the neurosciences, but it was actually initially developed as a method for signal processing and compression back in the 50s. So the work of Oliver Elias, which are actually contemporary of Shannon they realized that once we have a predictor, a model that works that is well in predicting data, sending messages about the error in those predictions is actually much cheaper than sending the entire message every time. And this is how predictive coding was born.', 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.07883808761835098, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.013532963581383228, 'label': 'Science'}, {'relevance': 0.007400310132652521, 'label': 'Religion&Spirituality>Astrology'}, {'relevance': 0.006787967402487993, 'label': 'Hobbies&Interests>ParanormalPhenomena'}, {'relevance': 0.005276068113744259, 'label': 'Science>Physics'}, {'relevance': 0.0014568812912330031, 'label': 'Education>EducationalAssessment'}, {'relevance': 0.0014308439567685127, 'label': 'Science>Genetics'}, {'relevance': 0.0010171046014875174, 'label': 'Education>EducationalAssessment>StandardizedTesting'}, {'relevance': 0.0009625407983548939, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.0007899558986537158, 'label': 'Science>SpaceAndAstronomy'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 242380, 'end': 285184}, 'sentences_idx_start': 25, 'sentences_idx_end': 27}, {'text': 'So as a signal processing and compression mechanism in information theory back in the 50s, it was actually in the 80s that exactly the same model was used in neuroscience with the work from Mumford or other works that, for example, explain how the Retina process information. So we get prediction signals from the outside world and we need to compress this representation and have this internal representation in our neurons. And the method is very similar, if not equivalent to the one that was developed by Elias and Oliver in the 50s.', 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.5946155786514282, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.06007697060704231, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.016566386446356773, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>BrainAndNervousSystemDisorders'}, {'relevance': 0.014600493013858795, 'label': 'Technology&Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.01185121014714241, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>EyeAndVisionConditions'}, {'relevance': 0.008921433240175247, 'label': 'Science'}, {'relevance': 0.004765171092003584, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Business>BusinessI.T.'}, {'relevance': 0.0029108065646141768, 'label': 'Science>Physics'}, {'relevance': 0.002188731450587511, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>Internet>ITAndInternetSupport'}, {'relevance': 0.0017484074924141169, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>VirtualReality'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 285232, 'end': 331270}, 'sentences_idx_start': 28, 'sentences_idx_end': 30}, {'text': "Maybe what's the biggest paradigm shift happened in 99 thanks to the work of Rao and Ballard in which they introduced this concept that I mentioned earlier about hierarchical structures in the brain where prediction information is top down and error information is bottom up. And something that they did that wasn't done before is that they explain and develop this theory about not only inference, but also about how learning works in the brain. So it's also a theory of how our synapses get updated.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.8581768274307251, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.01890852488577366, 'label': 'Science'}, {'relevance': 0.005223528947681189, 'label': 'FamilyAndRelationships>Parenting>ParentingChildrenAged4-11'}, {'relevance': 0.004582737106829882, 'label': 'Technology&Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.0037055888678878546, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.0022617769427597523, 'label': 'Education>EducationalAssessment'}, {'relevance': 0.0021518131252378225, 'label': 'Education>LanguageLearning'}, {'relevance': 0.0016910340636968613, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>BrainAndNervousSystemDisorders'}, {'relevance': 0.0015708915889263153, 'label': 'FamilyAndRelationships>Parenting>ParentingBabiesAndToddlers'}, {'relevance': 0.0010711264330893755, 'label': 'Education>HomeworkAndStudy'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 333000, 'end': 371600}, 'sentences_idx_start': 31, 'sentences_idx_end': 33}, {'text': "And the last big breakthrough that I'm going to talk about in this brief historical introduction is from 2003. But then he kept going in the years after, thanks to Karl Friston, in which basically he took the theory of Rowan Ballard and he developed, he extended it and generalized it to the theory of generative models. So basically the main claim that Karl Frieston did is that predictive coding is an evidence maximization scheme of a specific kind of generative model, which I'm going to introduce later as well.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.006719096098095179, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.005389199126511812, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.004445021040737629, 'label': 'Science'}, {'relevance': 0.0041410112753510475, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>DataStorageAndWarehousing'}, {'relevance': 0.0037993488367646933, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>Databases'}, {'relevance': 0.0011884900741279125, 'label': 'Science>Physics'}, {'relevance': 0.0010094190947711468, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>PersonalInvesting>Options'}, {'relevance': 0.0008019972592592239, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.0006721807876601815, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>PersonalInvesting'}, {'relevance': 0.0003501784522086382, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Business>BusinessOperations'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 373300, 'end': 414030}, 'sentences_idx_start': 34, 'sentences_idx_end': 36}, {'text': "So to make a brief summary, in the first two kinds of predica described so signal processing and compression and information processing in the Retina and in the brain in general, they are inference methods. And the biggest change, the biggest revolution that we had in 1999, so let's say in the 21st century, is apparative coding was seen as a learning algorithm.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.6300906538963318, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.45099034905433655, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>EyeAndVisionConditions'}, {'relevance': 0.054868150502443314, 'label': 'Technology&Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.04085134342312813, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.019302543252706528, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.006560391280800104, 'label': 'FamilyAndRelationships>Parenting>ParentingBabiesAndToddlers'}, {'relevance': 0.005845317617058754, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>3-DGraphics'}, {'relevance': 0.005190510768443346, 'label': "Style&Fashion>Women'sFashion>Women'sAccessories>Women'sGlasses"}, {'relevance': 0.0051094661466777325, 'label': 'Education>LanguageLearning'}, {'relevance': 0.0033439300023019314, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>AugmentedReality'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 415060, 'end': 444868}, 'sentences_idx_start': 37, 'sentences_idx_end': 38}, {'text': "So we can first compress information and then update all the synapses or all the latent variables that we have in our generative model to improve our generative model itself. So let's give some definitions that are a little bit more formal. So prior coding can be seen as a hierarchical Gaussian generative model. So here is a very simple figure in which we have this hierarchical structure which can be as deep as we want.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.4399300813674927, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.052237268537282944, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.024094145745038986, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>Databases'}, {'relevance': 0.01442056242376566, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>DataStorageAndWarehousing'}, {'relevance': 0.007766666356474161, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>3-DGraphics'}, {'relevance': 0.004368051886558533, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.002455025212839246, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>OperatingSystems'}, {'relevance': 0.0007001310004852712, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications'}, {'relevance': 0.0006695093470625579, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Robotics'}, {'relevance': 0.0006538962479680777, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Industries>BiotechAndBiomedicalIndustry'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 444964, 'end': 477750}, 'sentences_idx_start': 39, 'sentences_idx_end': 42}, {'text': "And prediction signals go from one latent variable XN to the following one and it gets transformed every time via function GN or GI. This is a generative model, as I said. And what's the marginal probability of this generative model? Well, it's simply the probability of the last can you see my cursor? Yes. Right? Yes. Perfect.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.003272391390055418, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>Databases'}, {'relevance': 0.0018026814796030521, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>DataStorageAndWarehousing'}, {'relevance': 0.0015839844709262252, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.0011352796573191881, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>PersonalInvesting>Options'}, {'relevance': 0.0007673099753446877, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>SimulationVideoGames'}, {'relevance': 0.0006822384893894196, 'label': 'Science>Physics'}, {'relevance': 0.0005584759637713432, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>3-DGraphics'}, {'relevance': 0.0005358564085327089, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.0004455030430108309, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Business>Sales'}, {'relevance': 0.0003968293312937021, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Economy>InterestRates'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 478360, 'end': 508262}, 'sentences_idx_start': 43, 'sentences_idx_end': 50}, {'text': "So it's the generative model of the last vertex is the probability distribution of the last vertex times the probability distribution of every other vertex conditioned on the activity of the vertex before or on the latent variable before. I earlier said that it's a Gaussian generative model, which means that those probabilities, they are in Gaussian form and every and those function g in general and especially since, for example, in Rambalar paper and in all the papers that came afterwards.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.0059123048558831215, 'label': 'Science>Physics'}, {'relevance': 0.002110081259161234, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>3-DGraphics'}, {'relevance': 0.0003410442441236228, 'label': 'Science>Chemistry'}, {'relevance': 0.0003263305698055774, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>Databases'}, {'relevance': 0.0002707337844185531, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.00023560844419989735, 'label': 'Science>SpaceAndAstronomy'}, {'relevance': 0.00020359482732601464, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>DataStorageAndWarehousing'}, {'relevance': 0.00017904792912304401, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>SimulationVideoGames'}, {'relevance': 0.00016628547746222466, 'label': 'Science>Genetics'}, {'relevance': 0.00011575807729968801, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>PuzzleVideoGames'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 508396, 'end': 546334}, 'sentences_idx_start': 51, 'sentences_idx_end': 52}, {'text': 'Also because of the deep learning revolution, those functions are simply linear maps or nonlinear maps with activation functions or nonlinear maps with activation function and an additive bias. So we can give an informal definition of creative coding and we can say predictive coding is an inversion scheme for such a generative model where its model evidence is maximized by minimizing a quantity that is called a variation of free energy. In general, the goal of every generative model is to maximize model evidence, but this quantity is always intractable.', 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.033815521746873856, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.0037703237030655146, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.0017025804845616221, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.0010490232380107045, 'label': 'Science>Physics'}, {'relevance': 0.0008243462070822716, 'label': 'Science'}, {'relevance': 0.0006024810718372464, 'label': 'Religion&Spirituality>Astrology'}, {'relevance': 0.0004798758018296212, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Business>BusinessOperations'}, {'relevance': 0.00037144191446714103, 'label': 'Education>EducationalAssessment'}, {'relevance': 0.0003689559525810182, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>3-DGraphics'}, {'relevance': 0.000281390268355608, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>PuzzleVideoGames'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 546462, 'end': 587950}, 'sentences_idx_start': 53, 'sentences_idx_end': 55}, {'text': "And we have some techniques that allow us to approximate this solution and the one that we use in pritef coding instead of minimizing aberration of free energy, which is a lower bound of the model evidence in this work and actually in a lot of other ones. So it's the standard way of doing it. This minimization is performed via gradient descent and yes, performed via gradient descent. And there are actually other methods such as expectation maximization which is often equivalent.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.010258246213197708, 'label': 'Science>Physics'}, {'relevance': 0.0032752403058111668, 'label': 'Science>SpaceAndAstronomy'}, {'relevance': 0.0008286466472782195, 'label': 'Automotive>AutoParts'}, {'relevance': 0.0006087830988690257, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>3-DGraphics'}, {'relevance': 0.0005004233680665493, 'label': 'Home&Garden>IndoorEnvironmentalQuality'}, {'relevance': 0.0004472919390536845, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>OperatingSystems'}, {'relevance': 0.00042936846148222685, 'label': 'Science>Chemistry'}, {'relevance': 0.0003506916109472513, 'label': 'NewsAndPolitics>Weather'}, {'relevance': 0.0003171572170685977, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.0002568670315667987, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 589170, 'end': 623398}, 'sentences_idx_start': 56, 'sentences_idx_end': 59}, {'text': 'Or you can use some other message passing algorithms such as belief propagation, for example, and going a little bit back in time. So forgetting a little bit about the statistical generative models, we can see pref coding, I said already a couple of times as a hierarchical model with neural activities. So with neurons, latent variables that represent neural activities, the sender signal down the hierarchy and with error nodes or error neurons, the sender signal up the hierarchy so they send the error information back.', 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.03572908416390419, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.005182998720556498, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.0030589697416871786, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.0012763597769662738, 'label': 'Science>Physics'}, {'relevance': 0.0007879486074671149, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>SimulationVideoGames'}, {'relevance': 0.0005193734541535378, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>Databases'}, {'relevance': 0.0004958201898261905, 'label': 'Science'}, {'relevance': 0.0004917026381008327, 'label': 'Science>Genetics'}, {'relevance': 0.0004448870604392141, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>EndocrineAndMetabolicDiseases>HormonalDisorders'}, {'relevance': 0.00041375684668309987, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>VirtualReality'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 623574, 'end': 662920}, 'sentences_idx_start': 60, 'sentences_idx_end': 62}, {'text': "What's the variation of free energy of these class operative coding models? It's simply the sum of the mean square error of all the error neurons, so is the sum of the total error squared. And this representation is going to be useful in the later slides and in how I'm going to explain how to use pref coding to model causal inference. For example, why do you think pre tip coding is important and is a nice algorithm to study?", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.027123846113681793, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>BrainAndNervousSystemDisorders'}, {'relevance': 0.01012911181896925, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>EndocrineAndMetabolicDiseases>HormonalDisorders'}, {'relevance': 0.0021707802079617977, 'label': 'Science>Genetics'}, {'relevance': 0.0012693469179794192, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>MedicalTests'}, {'relevance': 0.0011133505031466484, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.000836289837025106, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>SleepDisorders'}, {'relevance': 0.0004911128780804574, 'label': 'Science'}, {'relevance': 0.00040876632556319237, 'label': 'Science>Physics'}, {'relevance': 0.00034059814061038196, 'label': 'FamilyAndRelationships>Parenting>ParentingChildrenAged4-11'}, {'relevance': 0.00034027211950160563, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>EndocrineAndMetabolicDiseases'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 663610, 'end': 696740}, 'sentences_idx_start': 63, 'sentences_idx_end': 66}, {'text': 'Well, first of all, as I said earlier, it optimizes the correct objective which is the model evidence or marginal likelihood. And then it does so by optimizing a lower bound which is called the rational free energy, as I said. And the rational free energy is interesting because it can be written as a sum of two different terms which are and each of those term optimizing. It has important impacts, for example, in machine learning tasks or in general in learning tasks.', 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.021102799102663994, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.014302510768175125, 'label': 'Science>Physics'}, {'relevance': 0.004041490610688925, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.0028847819194197655, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.0014265519566833973, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Robotics'}, {'relevance': 0.0014106184244155884, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>SimulationVideoGames'}, {'relevance': 0.0005869549931958318, 'label': 'Science'}, {'relevance': 0.0005488429451361299, 'label': 'Science>Chemistry'}, {'relevance': 0.0004759525472763926, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>OperatingSystems'}, {'relevance': 0.000306045199977234, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Industries>BiotechAndBiomedicalIndustry'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 697430, 'end': 728670}, 'sentences_idx_start': 67, 'sentences_idx_end': 70}, {'text': "So one of those term forces memorization. So the second term basically forces the model to fit a specific data set and the first term forces the model to minimize the complexity. And as we know, for example from the Occam's razor theory, if we have two different models that perform similarly on a specific training set, the one that we have to get and the one that is expected to generalize the most is the less complex one.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.0031594065949320793, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.0011240156600251794, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.0008495373767800629, 'label': 'Education>EducationalAssessment'}, {'relevance': 0.0006830621277913451, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>Databases'}, {'relevance': 0.00046198134077712893, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>DataStorageAndWarehousing'}, {'relevance': 0.00043992698192596436, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.00038558783126063645, 'label': 'Science>Genetics'}, {'relevance': 0.000356452539563179, 'label': 'Science'}, {'relevance': 0.0003559570759534836, 'label': 'Science>Physics'}, {'relevance': 0.0003293828049208969, 'label': 'Shopping>SalesAndPromotions'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 729010, 'end': 760934}, 'sentences_idx_start': 71, 'sentences_idx_end': 73}, {'text': "So updating generative model via vibrational free energy allows us to basically converge to the optimal Occam razor model which both memorizes a data set but is also able to generalize very well on unsigned data points. A second reason of why prioritic coding is important is that it actually doesn't have to be defined on a hierarchical structure, but it can be modeled on more complex and flexible architectures such as directed graphical model with any shape or generalized even more to networks with a lot of cycles that resemble brain region.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.026678211987018585, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.020617898553609848, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.018646277487277985, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.01789998449385166, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>3-DGraphics'}, {'relevance': 0.0045679607428610325, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Robotics'}, {'relevance': 0.002830111887305975, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>Databases'}, {'relevance': 0.0023923784028738737, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>DataStorageAndWarehousing'}, {'relevance': 0.0023801946081221104, 'label': 'Science>Physics'}, {'relevance': 0.0007914044545032084, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>AugmentedReality'}, {'relevance': 0.0007511635194532573, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>SimulationVideoGames'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 761132, 'end': 805122}, 'sentences_idx_start': 74, 'sentences_idx_end': 75}, {'text': "And the underlying reason is that you're not learning and predicting with a forward pass and then back propagating the error but you're minimizing an energy function and this allows basically every kind of hierarchy to be allows to go behind hierarchies and allow to learn cycles. And this is actually quite important because the brain is full of cycles as we have some information from some recent papers that managed to map completely the brain of some animals such as fruit fly.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.0196041539311409, 'label': 'Science>BiologicalSciences'}, {'relevance': 0.006419419776648283, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.004232343286275864, 'label': 'Science>Genetics'}, {'relevance': 0.0011383433593437076, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Robotics'}, {'relevance': 0.0007530248258262873, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.0005722031928598881, 'label': 'Hobbies&Interests>Beekeeping'}, {'relevance': 0.0005062267882749438, 'label': 'Science'}, {'relevance': 0.00041266315383836627, 'label': 'Pets>Birds'}, {'relevance': 0.00029253048705868423, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>EndocrineAndMetabolicDiseases>HormonalDisorders'}, {'relevance': 0.00021427702449727803, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 805266, 'end': 840110}, 'sentences_idx_start': 76, 'sentences_idx_end': 77}, {'text': 'The brain is full of cycles so it makes sense to train our machine learning models or our models in general with an algorithm that allows us to train using cyclic structures. The third reason why prior coding is interesting is that it has been formally proven that it is more robust than standard neural networks during with back propagation. So if you have a neural network and you want to perform classification tasks, creative coding is more robust.', 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.3453688621520996, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.057245224714279175, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.011649481020867825, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.0006519827293232083, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Robotics'}, {'relevance': 0.000536135339