r': 'B'}, {'text': 'is', 'start': 2397612, 'end': 2397766, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'about', 'start': 2397788, 'end': 2398454, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'structure', 'start': 2398652, 'end': 2399234, 'confidence': 0.74871, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'learning.', 'start': 2399282, 'end': 2399880, 'confidence': 0.99997, 'speaker': 'B'}]}, {'text': "So structure learning, as I said, deals with the problem of learning the causal structure of the model from observational data. This is actually an old problem that has been around for decades and has always been until a couple of years ago tackled using combinatorial search methods. The problem with those combinatorial search methods is that their complexity grows double exponentially. So as soon as the data becomes multidimensional and the Bayesian graph that you want to learn grows in size learning, it it's incredibly slow. The new solution that came out actually a couple of years ago in a new research paper from 2018 showed that it's possible to actually learn this structure not using a combinatorial search method, but by using a gradient based method.", 'start': 2402410, 'end': 2461310, 'confidence': 0.99846, 'words': [{'text': 'So', 'start': 2402410, 'end': 2403110, 'confidence': 0.99846, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'structure', 'start': 2403260, 'end': 2403678, 'confidence': 0.69425, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'learning,', 'start': 2403714, 'end': 2403962, 'confidence': 0.99999, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'as', 'start': 2404016, 'end': 2404186, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'I', 'start': 2404208, 'end': 2404298, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'said,', 'start': 2404304, 'end': 2404762, 'confidence': 0.97035, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'deals', 'start': 2404896, 'end': 2405286, 'confidence': 0.9198, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'with', 'start': 2405318, 'end': 2405466, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'the', 'start': 2405488, 'end': 2405578, 'confid